  "query_preview": "..\n\nA strong upper-level ridge over the western Gulf Coast states will \nbe slow to break down over the next several days as it drifts east.\nThe southern Great Lakes will reside on the northern fringe of this \nridge. A weak and dry cold front looks to sink south tonight into \nMonday as expansive high pressure (1034 mb) builds over northern \nOntario. Not much cooler air will reach the southern Michigan \nborder, as 850 mb temps hold in the upper single digits to near 10 C \nearly next week.\n\nCooler air and light easterly flow will infiltrate the northern half \nof the CWA, which could be conducive for fog or low cloud \ndevelopment over the Thumb and Tri-Cities regions Monday night. For \ntonight, potential fog development looks to be confined to the \nsouthern half of the CWA. However, with light southwest flow ahead \nof the cold front, prospects appear much lower compared to the past \ncouple of days without the marine influence in play.\n\nToday marks the 9th consecutive dry day in a row, and it continues \nto look like we will be hard pressed to generate measurable \nrainfall. The best chance over the next 5 days looks to be on \nThursday, as Euro ensembles indicate chances increasing to at or \nabove 50 percent for a hundredth of an inch across the northern half \nof the CWA. With the upper-level wave and jet energy just beginning \nto track into the Gulf of Alaska at the present time, sticking with \nthe lower-chance PoPs of the NBM is reasonable.",
